{%- comment -%}
  PG Tile Copy: collapse the bundle ladder to one Buy 2 Get 1 Free tile.

  WHERE THE TILES ACTUALLY COME FROM. There are two tile builders in this
  theme and they look identical on screen:

    - pg-landing renders server-side tiles, data-pgx-tile="single"/"double".
    - pg-theme-css (window.pgLadder) renders .pgx-lad tiles from a TIERS array
      -- Buy 1 / Duo 25% / Trio 50% / Quad +1 FREE -- and HIDES pg-landing's
      with display:none.

  The ladder wins, so the ladder is what a shopper sees, and it is what has to
  change. An earlier pass rewrote pg-landing's tiles instead: correct code
  aimed at markup that is display:none on every product page.

  WHY NOT EDIT pg-theme-css. That file is 73KB and holds the storefront's
  entire visual layer. Its TIERS array is four lines of it. Rewriting the whole
  file through the API to change four lines risks the other 73KB for no reason,
  so the ladder is edited after it is built instead.

  WHAT THIS DOES on the orb and the wall art:

    - deletes the Trio and Quad tiles (tiers 2 and 3). Neither offer exists any
      more: the only discount on these products is Buy 2 Get 1 Free.
    - rewrites the Duo tile into that offer -- title, badge, chip, the free-unit
      line, and the price: two units paid against three at the single price.
    - makes it add THREE units.

  HOW THE THIRD UNIT IS ADDED. pgLadder's own add-to-cart counts the .pg-drop
  pickers inside the selected tile and multiplies by data-pg-mul. So there is
  nothing to intercept -- give the tile a third picker, or raise the
  multiplier, and it posts three units through pgLadder's existing path.

  Two shapes, because pgLadder builds the tile differently depending on how
  many variants a product has:

    - MANY variants (the orb): one picker per unit. A third picker is appended,
      built with window.pgDrop -- the same factory pgLadder uses. It is NOT
      cloned: .pg-drop carries a pgValue() method on the element, and
      cloneNode copies markup but not properties, so a cloned picker would
      throw the moment add-to-cart read it.
    - ONE variant (the wall art): a single picker, and data-pg-mul carries the
      count. The multiplier moves to 3 and pgLadder's x N badge is removed.

  Anything not on Buy 2 Get 1 Free -- the R36S with its own Duo Pack, any
  future product -- is left exactly as pgLadder built it.

  ------------------------------------------------------------------ VOLUME

  THE EXTRA VOLUME TIERS, ORB ONLY. Two more tiles are appended below the Buy 2
  Get 1 Free tile, and every tier on the ladder now quotes EXACTLY what the cart
  charges at the orb's $34.99 unit price:

      tile                orbs   cart charges   struck    save
      Single Item            1         $34.99    $70.00    50%
      Buy 2, Get 1 FREE      3         $69.98   $210.00    66%
      Buy 4, Get 2 FREE      6        $137.20   $420.00    67%
      Buy 5, Get 3 FREE      8        $174.95   $560.00    68%

  Single and Buy 2 Get 1 Free are still not touched for price by this file:
  those figures are pgLadder's own arithmetic on the variant price, so they
  follow the orb automatically. Only the BADGE on tier 1 is rewritten here.

  THE 6-PACK IS NOT A WHOLE NUMBER OF ORBS, DELIBERATELY. Every other rung is
  (paid units x unit price), because a buy-X-get-Y discount can only ever take
  WHOLE units off. Six orbs under a pure Buy 4 Get 2 is 4 x $34.99 = $139.96,
  and against $209.94 that reads SAVE 33% -- the identical rate to Buy 2 Get 1,
  because 2 free of 6 IS 1 free of 3. Two rungs reading the same number is what
  made the ladder look flat.

  So the 6-pack takes $72.74 off instead -- 2.08 orbs -- which lands on $137.20
  and reads 35%. That is exactly what the competitor charges, and inspecting
  their figure is where the number came from: their $72.74 is two free orbs plus
  $2.76, bought purely to move the badge off 33.

  The band is narrow: anything from $135.42 to $137.51 rounds to 35%. $137.20 is
  the competitor's own price, so it is the defensible point in that band.

  THIS RUNG NEEDS A DIFFERENT DISCOUNT TYPE. No BXGY can produce $137.20, so the
  store's "PokeOrb - Buy 4 Get 2 Free" is replaced by a fixed-amount automatic
  discount, $72.74 off six or more orbs. The old BXGY is DEACTIVATED rather than
  deleted, so this is one switch to reverse.

  (An earlier attempt dropped the orb to $33.55 so that 4 x 33.55 would land on
  $134.20 exactly. It was reverted: one unit price cannot satisfy two wanted
  figures -- 5 x 33.55 is $167.75, not $169.95 -- so it bought one of them at the
  cost of dropping the orb's price on every other surface of the shop. Discounting
  the bundle instead leaves the orb's own price alone.)

  WHY THE PRICES ARE STILL LITERAL HERE. They could be derived now, but the
  point of EXTRA is that a tier is a chosen offer, not a formula: change the
  free-unit ratio and the price follows from the deal, not from the code. They
  are in cents, and they are the one place to edit them. The struck-through
  figure IS derived, the same way tier 1 derives its own: the single price x
  the number of orbs in the pack.

  IF THE ORB PRICE MOVES AGAIN, both figures here have to move with it, or the
  tiles go back to disagreeing with the cart: 4 x unit and 5 x unit.

  WHAT THE SAVING IS MEASURED AGAINST. Both discounts a rung carries, COMBINED,
  by owner request: the free orbs and the per-orb markdown off the $70 MSRP
  together, so every badge is pay measured against compare-at x units. An
  earlier pass measured against units x the single price instead (the free
  orbs alone -- 33/35/38); the owner asked for the combined figure, which is
  also what the cart's own "You saved" line measures, so the badge a shopper
  taps on and the saving the cart then reports finally agree with each other.
  The struck figure on every rung is the same measure -- MSRP x units -- so the
  badge is exactly the arithmetic between the two prices printed beside it.

  Tier 0 now carries the same badge as everyone else -- SAVE 50%, its own MSRP
  markdown; it has no free units to add -- injected by ladder(), since
  pgLadder builds that tile badge-less.

  EVERY TILE STATES ITS SAVING, AND BEST DEAL SITS ON THE BIGGEST PACK. Tier 1
  used to carry "Best Deal!" and no number; it now carries "SAVE 66%" like the
  rest, and the 8-pack carries "BEST DEAL - SAVE 68%" -- the badge is the only
  place a tile can claim to be the best, and dropping the number to make room
  would have left the one rung a shopper is meant to land on as the only one
  not saying what it saves.

  WHY THE PERCENT IS FLOORED, NOT ROUNDED. Against MSRP the rungs land on
  50.0 / 66.7 / 67.3 / 68.8, and rounding collapses the middle two to 67 -- the
  ladder goes flat at exactly the rung the hand-tuned $137.20 price exists to
  lift (2 free of 6 IS 1 free of 3, so without the $2.76 sweetener they would
  tie outright). Flooring keeps every rung distinct -- 50 / 66 / 67 / 68 -- and
  a floored badge can only understate the saving, which is the safe direction
  for a printed claim.

  TIER NUMBERS 4 AND 5, NOT 2 AND 3. ladder() above deletes any tile whose
  data-pg-tier is '2' or '3' -- those are pgLadder's dead Trio and Quad offers.
  Numbering the new tiles 2 and 3 would have them built and deleted on
  alternating passes, about once a second.

  SELECTION HAS TO BE NORMALISED. pgLadder gives each tile it builds a click
  handler closed over ITS OWN array of tiles, so a click on one of those clears
  pgx-sel from pgLadder's tiles only -- a selected tile of ours would stay
  selected alongside it, and add-to-cart reads the FIRST selected tile in the
  document. One delegated listener on the document therefore owns selection for
  every .pgx-lad: it runs after pgLadder's own handler and leaves exactly one
  tile selected either way. Nothing else about add-to-cart changes -- the new
  tiles are ordinary .pgx-lad tiles with one .pg-drop per orb and
  data-pg-mul="1", so pgLadder's existing path posts 6 or 8 units unaided.

  THE CHECKOUT SIDE. pgLadder's own note says it: a tile promises a price that
  only a matching automatic discount can honour. Both discounts now exist on
  the store, created alongside these tiles:

      PokeOrb - Buy 2 Get 1 Free  BXGY, NO per-order limit, so it REPEATS:
                                  frees floor(n/3) orbs at any quantity
      PokeOrb - 6 Pack $137.20    amount-off, $72.74 off at 6+, once per order
      PokeOrb - Buy 5 Get 3 Free  BXGY, buy 5 get 3 at 100% off, once per order

  "PokeOrb - Buy 4 Get 2 Free" is DEACTIVATED. It was redundant even before the
  price change -- the repeating Buy 2 Get 1 already frees two orbs at six, so it
  produced the identical $139.96 -- and it now competes with the amount-off
  discount that replaced it.

  ONLY ONE PRODUCT DISCOUNT APPLIES PER CART LINE. This store is not Plus, and
  same-line stacking needs either Plus + productDiscountsWithTagsOnSameCartLine
  or a Discounts Allocator Function; neither is available, and the field is not
  even present in this store's DiscountCombinesWith. So these three do NOT add
  up -- Shopify picks the single best one for the shopper. That is why the
  6-pack discount is set combinesWith.productDiscounts:false: it makes the
  single-winner contest explicit rather than relying on it.

  THE WHOLE CURVE, COMPUTED AGAINST THOSE THREE (orbs -> what the cart bills):

      1  $34.99   2  $69.98   3  $69.98   4 $104.97   5 $139.96   6 $137.20
      7 $172.19   8 $174.95   9 $209.94  10 $244.93  11 $279.92  12 $279.92

  The three bundle tiles land exactly: 3 -> $69.98, 6 -> $137.20, 8 -> $174.95.

  TWO THINGS TO KNOW ABOUT THAT CURVE.

  First, six orbs cost LESS than five ($137.20 against $139.96). The sixth orb
  is free AND takes $2.76 off. That was already half true -- the repeating Buy 2
  Get 1 made the sixth orb free, so 5 and 6 both billed $139.96 -- and the
  amount-off deepens it by $2.76. Nobody should ever buy exactly five, and the
  cart upsell already walks them to six.

  Second, the 6-pack discount wins by a THIN margin and only in a narrow band.
  At six orbs it beats the repeating Buy 2 Get 1 by $72.74 - $69.98 = $2.76, and
  it wins only at 6, 7 and 8; from nine up Buy 2 Get 1 frees three orbs
  ($104.97) and takes over again. Shopify's "best for the customer" tie-break is
  observed behaviour rather than documented behaviour, so if the orb's price
  ever moves, re-check that $72.74 still beats floor(6/3) x unit, or the 6-pack
  silently reverts to $139.96 while the tile still promises $137.20.

  The standing "Extra 10% off entire order" is an ORDER-class discount and DOES
  combine on top of whichever product discount won, so the shopper actually pays
  $123.48 for six and $157.46 for eight -- under the tile, never over it, which
  is the safe direction and the same way every other rung behaves.

  ------------------------------------------------------------------ BUY BAR

  AND THE LADDER IS REPEATED IN THE STICKY BAR. pg-theme-css's #pg-sticky shows
  the selected tile's price and an ADD TO CART, with no way to change what is
  selected -- and it only appears once the real ladder has scrolled off, so on a
  nine-screen phone page the one control still visible silently sells whatever
  was picked by default. buybar() below adds a dropdown of every visible tile,
  and a second one mirroring the selected tile's own option select where it has
  one (the console's Storage). It lives here because these are the same tiles
  this file already owns. See the note above buybar() for how it drives the
  page's own controls rather than duplicating the add path.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  /* The note was 12.5px #A99BC0 -- a muted lavender at body weight, on the one
     line that tells a shopper what the tile they just picked actually does. It
     is white and one step heavier now; the highlighted phrase keeps the accent
     but goes brighter with it. */
  #pgx .pgx-deal-note{
    margin:4px 0 13px !important;
    font-size:13.5px;
    line-height:1.5;
    font-weight:600;
    color:#FFFFFF;
    text-align:center;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-deal-note b{color:#E4C4FF;font-weight:800}

  /* ---- the sticky buy bar's pack picker ----
     #pg-sticky is DARK under the pg-dark skin (rgba(11,7,16,.96)), not the
     white pg-theme-css paints, and pg_tile_copy loads after pg_dark in
     footer-group -- so the controls are styled for the dark bar. */
  /* THE BAR SHOWS WHAT IT IS SELLING. #pg-sticky is built by pg-theme-css as
     title + price + button; on a phone .pgst-t is display:none, so the bar was
     a price and a button with nothing identifying the product. The thumbnail is
     added from here rather than by editing pg-theme-css: that file assigns
     .pgst-in's innerHTML exactly once, inside its `if(!bar)` branch, and
     repaints only textContent afterwards -- so an element inserted alongside
     the picker row survives every repaint, and the 73KB shared file stays
     untouched. */
  #pg-sticky .pgst-thumb{
    flex:0 0 auto;width:46px;height:46px;border-radius:10px;object-fit:cover;
    background:rgba(139,68,190,.16);
    box-shadow:0 0 0 1px rgba(190,140,235,.45);
  }
  @media(max-width:430px){ #pg-sticky .pgst-thumb{width:42px;height:42px} }

  #pg-sticky .pgs-pick{
    display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;
    /* WRAPS RATHER THAN CRUSHES. Side by side on a 390px phone the two fields
       squeezed the storage select to ~45px -- "128GB - 10,000 games" rendered
       as "12...", and storage is the thing being chosen. The flex-basis values
       below are wider than half a phone, so a second field drops to its own
       line there and both stay readable; on the orb, where there is no second
       field, the pack still takes the whole row exactly as before. */
    flex-wrap:wrap;
    max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto 9px;
    font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;
  }
  #pg-sticky .pgs-pick .pgs-f{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;min-width:0}
  #pg-sticky .pgs-pick .pgs-f-pack{flex:2 1 240px}
  #pg-sticky .pgs-pick .pgs-f-opt{flex:1 1 170px}
  #pg-sticky .pgs-pick .pgs-f > span{
    flex:none;
    font-size:10.5px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;
    color:#C79BEA;
  }
  #pg-sticky .pgs-pick select{
    flex:1 1 auto;min-width:0;width:100%;
    -webkit-appearance:menulist;appearance:menulist;
    padding:8px 10px;
    border:1.5px solid rgba(190,140,235,.55);border-radius:10px;
    background:#16101F;color:#EFE8F7;
    font-family:inherit;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2;
  }
  #pg-sticky .pgs-pick select option{background:#16101F;color:#EFE8F7}
  #pg-sticky .pgs-pick select:focus{outline:2px solid #9B4BC4;outline-offset:1px}
  /* NO PICKER ROW ON PHONES, by owner request. On a 390px screen the row sat
     as its own sticky band above the price + ADD TO CART bar, and the two
     stacked bands ate a fifth of the viewport; the ladder itself is one swipe
     up. Hidden by CSS rather than skipped in buybar(): the JS keeps the row
     built and synced, so rotating a phone to a wide screen shows a correct
     picker instantly and nothing else needs to know about the breakpoint. */
  @media(max-width:760px){
    #pg-sticky .pgs-pick{display:none !important}
  }
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  if (window.pgTileCopy) return; window.pgTileCopy = 1;

  var FROM = /^buy\s*1$/i;
  var TO = 'Single Item';
  var TILES = '#pgx .pgx-tile, #pgx [class*="pgx-lad"]';

  /* Products running Buy 2 Get 1 Free. */
  var B2G1 = {
    'pokemon-crystal-ball-3d-pikachu-gengar-mewtwo-night-light-lamp-pokeball-3d-glass-pokemon-crystal-ball-statue-kids-birthday-gift': {noun: 'Orb', chip: '+3 FREE LED Bases'},
    'anime-pokemon-3d-lenticular-poster-charizard-squirtle-bulbasaur-come-alive-with-amazing-3d-effects-wall-art-printwithout-frame': {noun: 'Print', chip: '+3 PRINTS'}
  };

  function handle(){
    var m = location.pathname.match(/\/products\/([^\/?#]+)/);
    return m ? m[1] : '';
  }
  var HANDLE = handle();
  var PACK = B2G1[HANDLE] || null;

  /* The extra volume tiers, orb only. `pay` is the price shown, in cents, and
     is what the cart actually charges. The 8-pack is 5 paid orbs at $34.99; the
     6-pack is NOT a whole number of orbs -- it is $72.74 off six, which is why
     it needs an amount-off discount rather than a buy-X-get-Y (see the note at
     the top of the file). If the unit price ever moves, BOTH figures here have
     to move with it and the discount has to be re-cut, or the tiles stop
     agreeing with the cart.
     `units` is how many orbs the tile adds, and how many pickers it draws. The
     struck-through figure is NOT here: it is the single price x units, derived
     at render time so it matches the tile above. */
  var EXTRA_ON = HANDLE.indexOf('pokemon-crystal') !== -1;
  var EXTRA = [
    {tier: '4', units: 6, free: 2, pay: 13720, title: 'Buy 4, Get 2 FREE', chip: '+6 FREE LED Bases'},
    {tier: '5', units: 8, free: 3, pay: 17495, title: 'Buy 5, Get 3 FREE', chip: '+8 FREE LED Bases', best: true}
  ];

  /* THE NOTE FOLLOWS THE TILE. It used to be one fixed sentence -- "Buy 2, get
     your 3rd free" -- printed under Add to Cart whichever rung was selected, so
     picking the 8-pack left a line describing a different offer sitting under
     the button. It is derived from the selected tile now, and rebuilt whenever
     selection changes (see ownSelection). Tier 0 has no offer of its own, so it
     points at the first one instead of saying nothing. */
  function noteFor(tier){
    if (!PACK) return 'Bundle pricing shown. Your <b>best available discount</b> is applied automatically at checkout.';
    var n = PACK.noun.toLowerCase();
    if (tier === '1'){
      return 'Buy 2, get your <b>3rd ' + n + ' free</b> &mdash; the free one comes off automatically at checkout.';
    }
    for (var i = 0; i < EXTRA.length; i++){
      if (EXTRA[i].tier === tier){
        var E = EXTRA[i];
        return 'Buy ' + (E.units - E.free) + ', get <b>' + E.free + ' ' + n + 's free</b>'
             + ' &mdash; the free ones come off automatically at checkout.';
      }
    }
    return 'Add 1 more and your <b>3rd ' + n + ' is free</b> &mdash; it comes off automatically at checkout.';
  }

  function money(c){ return '$' + (c / 100).toFixed(2); }

  /* What a tile saves against MSRP x units -- the free orbs AND the per-orb
     markdown counted together, by owner request, so the badge agrees with the
     cart's own "You saved" line, which measures the same way. One helper so
     every rung is measured the same way and none of them can drift.

     FLOOR, not round. Against MSRP the 3-pack lands on 66.7% and the 6-pack on
     67.3%; rounding makes both read 67 and the ladder goes flat at exactly the
     rung the hand-tuned $137.20 exists to lift. Flooring keeps every rung
     distinct -- 50 / 66 / 67 / 68 -- and only ever understates, which is the
     safe direction for a claim printed on a price. */
  function pctOff(pay, full){
    return full > pay ? Math.floor((full - pay) / full * 100) : 0;
  }

  /* MSRP in cents, falling back to the price itself when a product carries no
     compare-at -- the wall art's badge then measures free prints alone. */
  function capOf(v){
    return (v.cap && v.cap > v.price) ? v.cap : v.price;
  }

  function swapIn(root){
    if (!root || root.nodeType !== 1) return;
    var walker = document.createTreeWalker(root, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT, null);
    var hits = [], n;
    while ((n = walker.nextNode())){
      if (FROM.test((n.nodeValue || '').trim())) hits.push(n);
    }
    hits.forEach(function(t){ t.nodeValue = t.nodeValue.replace(/buy\s*1/i, TO); });
    return hits.length > 0;
  }

  var variants = null;
  function vdata(){
    if (variants) return variants;
    var el = document.getElementById('pgx-vdata');
    if (!el) return null;
    try { variants = JSON.parse(el.textContent); } catch(e){ variants = null; }
    return variants;
  }

  /* ---------------- picker options ----------------
     #pgx-vdata carries id / size / price / cap and NO images, which is fine
     for pricing and wrong for a picker: pgDrop renders a thumbnail per row, so
     options built from vdata gave the third picker a column of broken-image
     icons and a list wide enough to need a horizontal scrollbar.

     So the options come from the same place pgLadder gets its own -- the
     product JSON, with each variant's featured image and the product's first
     image as the fallback. Fetched once and cached; ladder() runs on an
     interval, so the picker is built on whichever pass the fetch lands by. */
  var VOPTS = null, optsBusy = false;
  function loadOpts(){
    if (VOPTS || optsBusy || !HANDLE) return VOPTS;
    optsBusy = true;
    fetch('/products/' + HANDLE + '.js').then(function(r){ return r.json(); }).then(function(p){
      var fallback = (p.images && p.images[0]) || '';
      VOPTS = (p.variants || []).filter(function(v){ return v.available !== false; })
        .map(function(v){
          return {
            id: v.id,
            t: v.title,
            img: (v.featured_image && v.featured_image.src) || fallback
          };
        });
      optsBusy = false;
    }).catch(function(){ optsBusy = false; });
    return VOPTS;
  }

  function put(el, text){
    if (el && el.textContent !== text) el.textContent = text;
  }

  /* ---------------- the ladder ---------------- */

  function ladder(){
    if (!PACK) return;
    var tiles = document.querySelectorAll('#pgx .pgx-lad');
    if (!tiles.length) return;                 /* pgLadder has not built yet */

    /* 1. the offers that no longer exist */
    tiles.forEach(function(t){
      var tier = t.getAttribute('data-pg-tier');
      if (tier === '2' || tier === '3'){
        /* if the shopper had one of them selected, hand selection back to Buy 1 */
        var wasSel = t.classList.contains('pgx-sel');
        if (t.parentNode) t.parentNode.removeChild(t);
        if (wasSel){
          var first = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-lad[data-pg-tier="0"]');
          if (first){ first.classList.add('pgx-sel'); first.setAttribute('aria-checked', 'true'); }
        }
      }
    });

    /* 2. the pack tile */
    var tile = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-lad[data-pg-tier="1"]');
    if (!tile) return;

    var vs = vdata();
    if (!vs || !vs.length) return;
    var unit = vs[0].price;
    var cap = capOf(vs[0]);

    /* THE SINGLE STATES ITS SAVING TOO. pgLadder builds tier 0 with no badge
       at all, and the owner circled the empty corner: with every bundle badge
       now measured against MSRP, the single's own 50% off MSRP is the same
       claim and belongs on the same corner. Injected once; put() keeps the
       repaint silent on every later pass. */
    var single = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-lad[data-pg-tier="0"]');
    if (single && cap > unit){
      var sb = single.querySelector('.pgx-lad-badge');
      if (!sb){
        sb = document.createElement('span');
        sb.className = 'pgx-lad-badge';
        single.insertBefore(sb, single.firstChild);
      }
      put(sb, 'SAVE ' + pctOff(unit, cap) + '%');
    }

    put(tile.querySelector('.pgx-lad-t b'), 'Buy 2, Get 1 FREE');
    put(tile.querySelector('.pgx-lad-chip'), PACK.chip);
    put(tile.querySelector('.pgx-lad-save'), '3rd ' + PACK.noun + ' = FREE!');
    /* BEST DEAL BELONGS TO THE BIGGEST PACK, so this tile states what it saves
       instead, like every other rung. Same arithmetic the new tiles use: what
       is paid against MSRP for the same number of orbs. The struck figure is
       the same measure, so the badge is the arithmetic between the two prices
       printed beside it. */
    put(tile.querySelector('.pgx-lad-badge'), 'SAVE ' + pctOff(unit * 2, cap * 3) + '%');
    put(tile.querySelector('.pgx-lad-px b'), money(unit * 2));
    put(tile.querySelector('.pgx-lad-px s'), money(cap * 3));

    /* 3. three units, through pgLadder's own add-to-cart */
    var picks = tile.querySelector('.pgx-lad-picks');
    if (!picks) return;
    var drops = picks.querySelectorAll('.pg-drop');

    if (drops.length > 1){
      /* one picker per unit: add the third, built the way pgLadder builds them */
      if (drops.length >= 3) return;
      if (typeof window.pgDrop !== 'function') return;   /* no factory, no guess */
      var opts = loadOpts();
      if (!opts || !opts.length) return;                 /* still loading: next pass */
      var row = document.createElement('div');
      row.className = 'pgx-lad-row';
      row.appendChild(window.pgDrop(opts, opts[2 % opts.length].id, null));
      picks.appendChild(row);
      tile.dataset.pgMul = '1';
    } else if (drops.length === 1){
      /* Single design (the wall art): pgLadder gives the tile ONE picker and
         carries the count on data-pg-mul, with a x N badge beside it.

         The multiplier moves to 3 -- that is what actually decides how many
         units add-to-cart posts. The badge is REMOVED rather than relabelled:
         it is the only place in the tile that repeats the count, the title and
         the "+3 PRINTS" chip already say it, and while it was drawn by
         pgLadder it kept showing the old x2 next to an offer that gives three.
         One fewer number, and no chance of it disagreeing with the cart. */
      tile.dataset.pgMul = '3';
      var pickRow = picks.querySelector('.pgx-lad-row');
      if (pickRow){
        var xb = pickRow.querySelector('b');
        if (xb && xb.parentNode) xb.parentNode.removeChild(xb);
      }
    }
  }

  /* ---------------- the extra volume tiers ----------------

     Built exactly the way pgLadder builds its own -- same classes, same head /
     price / picks structure, same picker factory -- so they inherit the
     ladder's styling and its add-to-cart without either being told about them.
     Appended after the last ladder tile, which is the Buy 2 Get 1 Free tile
     once ladder() has removed the dead ones, so the order on screen reads
     Single, Buy 2 Get 1, Buy 4 Get 2, Buy 5 Get 3.

     Everything here is a no-op unless the pack tile is on screen: if pgLadder
     tears the ladder down (it rebuilds whenever no .pgx-lad is left) these
     tiles go with it, because leaving them standing is what would stop it
     rebuilding at all -- its own guard is `if (document.querySelector('.pgx-lad')) return`. */

  function ord(k){
    var t = k % 10, h = k % 100;
    if (t === 1 && h !== 11) return k + 'st';
    if (t === 2 && h !== 12) return k + 'nd';
    if (t === 3 && h !== 13) return k + 'rd';
    return k + 'th';
  }

  /* "5th & 6th Orb = FREE!", "6th, 7th & 8th Orb = FREE!" -- the same sentence
     tier 1 gets from ladder(), widened to name more than one free unit. */
  function freeLine(units, free, noun){
    var parts = [];
    for (var i = units - free + 1; i <= units; i++) parts.push(ord(i));
    var last = parts.pop();
    return (parts.length ? parts.join(', ') + ' & ' : '') + last + ' ' + noun + ' = FREE!';
  }

  function extraTile(E, unit, cap, opts){
    var full = cap * E.units;
    var pct = pctOff(E.pay, full);
    /* The biggest pack carries BEST DEAL and its percentage together: the badge
       is the only place a tile can claim to be the best, and dropping the
       number to make room would have left the one rung a shopper is meant to
       land on as the only one not saying what it saves. */
    var badge = E.best ? 'BEST DEAL &middot; SAVE ' + pct + '%' : 'SAVE ' + pct + '%';

    var tile = document.createElement('div');
    tile.className = 'pgx-tile pgx-lad';
    tile.setAttribute('role', 'radio');
    tile.setAttribute('aria-checked', 'false');
    tile.setAttribute('tabindex', '0');
    tile.setAttribute('data-pg-tier', E.tier);
    tile.setAttribute('data-pg-extra', '1');
    tile.dataset.pgMul = '1';

    tile.innerHTML = (pct > 0 ? '<span class="pgx-lad-badge">' + badge + '</span>' : '')
      + '<div class="pgx-lad-head"><span class="pgx-duo-dot"></span>'
      + '<div class="pgx-lad-t"><b>' + E.title + '</b>'
      + '<span class="pgx-lad-chip">' + E.chip + '</span>'
      + '<div class="pgx-lad-save">' + freeLine(E.units, E.free, PACK.noun) + '</div></div>'
      + '<div class="pgx-lad-px"><b>' + money(E.pay) + '</b><s>' + money(full) + '</s></div></div>'
      + '<div class="pgx-lad-picks"><div class="lbl">Choose your Poke Balls</div></div>';

    /* One picker per orb, built with pgLadder's own factory rather than cloned
       -- .pg-drop carries pgValue() as a property on the element, and cloneNode
       copies markup but not properties, so a cloned picker throws the moment
       add-to-cart reads it. */
    var picks = tile.querySelector('.pgx-lad-picks');
    for (var i = 0; i < E.units; i++){
      var row = document.createElement('div');
      row.className = 'pgx-lad-row';
      row.appendChild(window.pgDrop(opts, opts[i % opts.length].id, null));
      picks.appendChild(row);
    }
    return tile;
  }

  function extras(){
    if (!EXTRA_ON || !PACK) return;

    var pack = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-lad[data-pg-tier="1"]');
    if (!pack){
      /* no pack tile means no ladder (or one mid-rebuild): ours must not
         outlive it, or pgLadder never rebuilds */
      document.querySelectorAll('#pgx .pgx-lad[data-pg-extra]').forEach(function(t){
        if (t.parentNode) t.parentNode.removeChild(t);
      });
      return;
    }

    if (typeof window.pgDrop !== 'function') return;
    var vs = vdata();
    if (!vs || !vs.length) return;
    var unit = vs[0].price;
    var cap = capOf(vs[0]);
    var opts = loadOpts();
    if (!opts || !opts.length) return;                  /* still loading: next pass */

    EXTRA.forEach(function(E){
      if (document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-lad[data-pg-tier="' + E.tier + '"]')) return;
      var all = document.querySelectorAll('#pgx .pgx-lad');
      var after = all[all.length - 1];
      if (!after || !after.parentNode) return;
      after.parentNode.insertBefore(extraTile(E, unit, cap, opts), after.nextSibling);
    });
  }

  /* ONE OWNER FOR SELECTION. pgLadder's per-tile handler is closed over the
     array of tiles IT built, so it cannot clear pgx-sel from ours and ours
     could not clear it from its. Add-to-cart reads the first selected tile in
     the document, so two selected tiles silently charge for the wrong one.
     This delegated listener runs after whichever handler fired and leaves
     exactly one .pgx-lad selected, whoever built it. */
  function ownSelection(){
    document.addEventListener('click', function(e){
      if (!e.target || !e.target.closest) return;
      var hit = e.target.closest('#pgx .pgx-lad');
      if (!hit) return;
      document.querySelectorAll('#pgx .pgx-lad').forEach(function(t){
        var on = t === hit;
        t.classList.toggle('pgx-sel', on);
        t.setAttribute('aria-checked', on ? 'true' : 'false');
      });
      note();          /* the sentence under the button belongs to the new tile */
      buybar();        /* and so does the bar's pack dropdown */
    });
  }

  function note(){
    var root = document.getElementById('pgx');
    if (!root) return;
    if (!root.querySelector('.pgx-tile, [class*="pgx-lad"]')) return;
    var atc = root.querySelector('.pgx-atc');
    if (!atc || !atc.parentNode) return;
    var p = root.querySelector('.pgx-deal-note');
    if (!p){
      p = document.createElement('p');
      p.className = 'pgx-deal-note';
      atc.parentNode.insertBefore(p, atc);
    }
    var sel = root.querySelector('.pgx-lad.pgx-sel') || root.querySelector('.pgx-tile.pgx-sel');
    var html = noteFor(sel ? (sel.getAttribute('data-pg-tier') || '') : '');
    /* Compare against what we last WROTE, not against innerHTML read back:
       the parser turns `&mdash;` into a literal em dash, so re-reading never
       equals the source string, every pass would rewrite the node, and the
       MutationObserver below would call pass() again -- forever. The record
       lives on a JS property so keeping it is not itself a DOM mutation. */
    if (p.pgNote !== html){ p.pgNote = html; p.innerHTML = html; }
  }

  /* ---------------- the sticky buy bar's pack picker ----------------

     pg-theme-css builds #pg-sticky as title / price / ADD TO CART. It MIRRORS
     the selected tile's price but offers no way to change that selection, and
     the product page is nine screens tall on a phone: by the time the bar
     appears the ladder is far above it, so the one control still on screen
     silently sells whichever tile was picked -- in practice the single, because
     that is what pgLadder selects by default. The bundles were invisible at
     exactly the point the decision gets made.

     So the bar gets one row above it: a <select> naming every visible tile with
     its price and its saving, and -- for products whose tile carries native
     option selects (the console's Storage) -- a second mirroring that one.
     DESKTOP ONLY: on phones the row is hidden outright (see the media rule
     above) -- the owner found two stacked sticky bands too tall for a 390px
     screen, and the thumbnail + price still say what the button sells.

     IT DRIVES THE PAGE'S OWN CONTROLS, IT DOES NOT REPLACE THEM. Choosing a
     pack CLICKS the tile, so pgLadder's per-tile handler and ownSelection()
     above both run and add-to-cart posts that pack unaided; the option select
     writes to the page's select and fires a bubbling change, which is what
     pgLadder's own duo handler listens for. Nothing about the add path is
     duplicated here, so it cannot drift from it. */

  function esc(t){ return String(t).replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;'); }

  /* pgLadder hides the server-rendered tiles with an inline display:none when
     it builds its own, so ask the layout which tiles are real rather than
     trying to reproduce that rule here. */
  function visibleTiles(){
    var out = [];
    document.querySelectorAll('#pgx .pgx-tile').forEach(function(t){
      if (t.getClientRects().length) out.push(t);
    });
    return out;
  }

  function tileLabel(t){
    var name = t.querySelector('.pgx-lad-t b') || t.querySelector('.pgx-tile-name') ||
               t.querySelector('.pgx-tile-row b') || t.querySelector('b');
    var px = t.querySelector('.pgx-lad-px b') || t.querySelector('.pgx-tile-price');
    var badge = t.querySelector('.pgx-lad-badge');
    var out = name ? name.textContent.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim() : 'Option';
    var amt = px ? (px.textContent.match(/\$[0-9][0-9.,]*/) || [])[0] : '';
    if (amt) out += ' - ' + amt;
    /* the saving rides along: it is the whole reason to pick a bigger pack,
       and the badge is where every tier already states it */
    if (badge){
      var b = badge.textContent.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
      if (b) out += ' (' + b + ')';
    }
    return out;
  }

  /* The console carries Storage on a select of its own, built by pgLadder's
     single-tile path and living outside the tile; anything else uses the first
     select inside the tile that is actually selected. The orb's pickers are
     .pg-drop widgets, not selects, so they are never matched here -- eight
     design dropdowns do not belong in a bottom bar anyway. */
  function optSource(){
    var st = document.querySelector('#pgx select.pgx-storage');
    if (st && st.getClientRects().length) return st;
    var sel = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-tile.pgx-sel');
    if (!sel) return null;
    var s = sel.querySelector('select');
    return (s && s.getClientRects().length) ? s : null;
  }

  var bbRow = null, bbPack = null, bbOpt = null, bbSig = '';

  function buybar(){
    var bar = document.getElementById('pg-sticky');
    if (!bar) return;                        /* not a product page yet */
    var inRow = bar.querySelector('.pgst-in');
    if (!inRow || !inRow.parentNode) return;

    if (!bbRow || !bbRow.isConnected){
      bbRow = document.createElement('div');
      bbRow.className = 'pgs-pick';
      bbRow.innerHTML =
        '<label class="pgs-f pgs-f-pack"><span>Bundle</span>' +
        '<select class="pgs-pack" aria-label="Choose your bundle"></select></label>' +
        '<label class="pgs-f pgs-f-opt"><span class="pgs-opt-lb">Option</span>' +
        '<select class="pgs-opt" aria-label="Choose an option"></select></label>';
      bbPack = bbRow.querySelector('.pgs-pack');
      bbOpt = bbRow.querySelector('.pgs-opt');
      bbSig = '';

      bbPack.addEventListener('change', function(){
        var t = visibleTiles()[parseInt(bbPack.value, 10)];
        if (t) t.click();                    /* the page's own selection path */
        buybar();
      });
      bbOpt.addEventListener('change', function(){
        var src = optSource();
        if (!src || src.value === bbOpt.value) return;
        src.value = bbOpt.value;
        try { src.dispatchEvent(new Event('change', {bubbles: true})); }
        catch(e){
          var ev = document.createEvent('HTMLEvents');
          ev.initEvent('change', true, false);
          src.dispatchEvent(ev);
        }
      });
      inRow.parentNode.insertBefore(bbRow, inRow);
    }

    /* the thumbnail lives in the price/button row, ahead of the title */
    var thumb = inRow.querySelector('.pgst-thumb');
    if (!thumb){
      thumb = document.createElement('img');
      thumb.className = 'pgst-thumb';
      thumb.alt = '';
      thumb.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      inRow.insertBefore(thumb, inRow.firstChild);
    }
    /* follow the shopper's own choice where there is one: the selected tile's
       first design picker carries the character they picked, and that is what
       is going in the bag. Fall back to the gallery photo on screen, then to
       the first strip image, so the bar is never left with a broken frame. */
    var pick = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-tile.pgx-sel .pgx-lad-picks .pg-drop img')
            || document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-thumbs button img')
            || document.querySelector('#pgx img');
    var psrc = pick && pick.getAttribute('src');
    if (psrc && thumb.getAttribute('src') !== psrc) thumb.setAttribute('src', psrc);
    /* shown on whether it HAS a picture, not on whether this tick found one.
       buybar() runs on a timer against a ladder that is rebuilt underneath it,
       so a tick can land in the gap where the selected tile's pickers do not
       exist yet -- keyed off psrc the bar would blink its product image out and
       back on every one of those. The last good frame stays up instead. */
    thumb.style.display = thumb.getAttribute('src') ? '' : 'none';

    /* ---- the packs ---- */
    var tiles = visibleTiles();
    var packF = bbRow.querySelector('.pgs-f-pack');
    if (tiles.length > 1){
      packF.style.display = '';
      var sig = tiles.map(tileLabel).join('|');
      if (sig !== bbSig){
        bbSig = sig;
        bbPack.innerHTML = tiles.map(function(t, i){
          return '<option value="' + i + '">' + esc(tileLabel(t)) + '</option>';
        }).join('');
      }
      var at = 0;
      for (var i = 0; i < tiles.length; i++){
        if (tiles[i].classList.contains('pgx-sel')){ at = i; break; }
      }
      if (bbPack.value !== String(at)) bbPack.value = String(at);
    } else {
      packF.style.display = 'none';          /* one tile: nothing to choose */
      bbSig = '';
    }

    /* ---- the selected pack's own options ---- */
    var src = optSource();
    var optF = bbRow.querySelector('.pgs-f-opt');
    if (!src){
      optF.style.display = 'none';
      bbOpt.pgSig = '';
      return;
    }
    optF.style.display = '';
    var osig = Array.prototype.map.call(src.options, function(o){
      return o.value + ':' + o.textContent;
    }).join('|');
    if (bbOpt.pgSig !== osig){
      bbOpt.pgSig = osig;
      bbOpt.innerHTML = src.innerHTML;
    }
    if (bbOpt.value !== src.value) bbOpt.value = src.value;
    var lb = bbRow.querySelector('.pgs-opt-lb');
    var lbt = src.getAttribute('aria-label') || 'Option';
    if (lb.textContent !== lbt) lb.textContent = lbt;
  }

  function pass(){
    document.querySelectorAll(TILES).forEach(function(tile){
      if (tile.dataset.pgCopy) return;
      if (swapIn(tile)) tile.dataset.pgCopy = '1';
    });
    ladder();
    extras();
    note();
    buybar();
  }

  function boot(){
    ownSelection();
    pass();
    var root = document.getElementById('pgx');
    if (root){
      try { new MutationObserver(pass).observe(root, {childList: true, subtree: true}); } catch(e){}
    }
    /* pgLadder rebuilds on its own 3s timer whenever .pgx-lad is missing, so
       this keeps pace with it rather than running once */
    setInterval(pass, 1200);
  }

  if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', boot); else boot();
})();
</script>
{% schema %}
{"name": "PG Tile Copy", "settings": []}
{% endschema %}
